---
/** Getting started — deliberately the simplest section on the page: the install
 * path this project actually uses, with the current release resolved at build
 * time (never typed by hand). `@latest` in the command is the point: it cannot
 * go stale between releases, so no instruction can tell a user to install an
 * outdated version. */
import { readPluginVersion } from '../data/latest';
const { version } = readPluginVersion();
---
<section class="dsh-start" id="getting-started">
  <div class="dsh-inner" style="padding-inline:0">
    <div class="dsh-section-title"><h2>Getting started</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step">
        <div class="dsh-step">
          <h3>Install</h3>
          <p class="note">Adds dsh-chapters to your harness profile — a plain npm directory — and brings its six tools, seven commands, and both archive buttons with it. The release this site documents: <code>{version}</code>.</p>
          <div class="dsh-cmdline">
            <code id="cmd-add">npm i @treeseed/dsh-chapters@latest</code>
            <button class="dsh-copy" type="button" data-cmd="#cmd-add">copy</button>
          </div>
          <p class="note">Run it in the profile directory (<code>~/.dsh/profiles/&lt;profile&gt;/</code>). <a href="./operations/install/">§23</a> covers every path.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div class="dsh-step">
          <h3>Keep it current</h3>
          <p class="note">The same line re-run moves the profile to whatever npm calls <code>latest</code> — no number to chase, so no stale instruction is possible.</p>
          <div class="dsh-cmdline">
            <code id="cmd-update">npm i @treeseed/dsh-chapters@latest</code>
            <button class="dsh-copy" type="button" data-cmd="#cmd-update">copy</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div class="dsh-step">
          <h3>Restart, then go</h3>
          <p class="note">Restart <code>dsh web</code> so the bundle loads. From any session: click <em>Continue with chapters</em> on a message to carry the work on, or <em>Fork with chapters</em> to branch it — one deterministic door, two intents. That’s the whole setup.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--sl-color-gray-3);margin-top:1rem">
      A profile is just a <code>package.json</code> plus its <code>node_modules</code> under
      <code>&lt;DSH home&gt;/profiles/&lt;name&gt;/</code> — installing is npm, updating is npm again.
      The archive stays yours: plain Markdown, kept on uninstall.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('click', async (ev) => {
    const btn = ev.target.closest ? ev.target.closest('.dsh-copy') : null;
    if (!btn) return;
    const code = document.querySelector(btn.dataset.cmd);
    if (!code) return;
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.textContent.trim()); btn.textContent = 'copied ✓'; btn.classList.add('done');
      setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = 'copy'; btn.classList.remove('done'); }, 1600); } catch (e) {}
  });
</script>
